-- title:
--   Cyclic inertia of odd order at a supersingular point, p=2
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $p=2$, and let $M\ge 5$ with $p\nmid M$ and such that some prime $\ell\mid M$ satisfies $\ell\not\equiv 1\pmod 4$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a $p$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb Q$, with $\zeta\in L$ a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Inside $L$-Laurent series, let $K_1$ be the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion field $\mathbb Q$-generated by the integral form ratios for $\Gamma_1(M)\cap\Gamma_0(p)$, and $K_2$ the corresponding field for $\Gamma_0(Mp)$, with $K_2\le K_1$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$, with $p$ in its maximal ideal, $\zeta$ in the image of $A$, uniformiser $\varpi$ generating the maximal ideal, and with compatible algebra structures on $K_1$, $K_2$. Let $j\in K_1$ and $j_2\in K_2$ be nonzero elements whose Laurent expansions are both the image of the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}\cdot(\text{power series } jNumQ)$ of the modular invariant. The hypotheses then supply a morphism $\pi_2$ of the two-chart integral models $\mathrm{TwoChartIntegralModel}\,A\,K_1\,j\to \mathrm{TwoChartIntegralModel}\,A\,K_2\,j_2$ (each the pushout gluing the spectra of the integral closures of $A[j]$ and $A[j^{-1}]$ in the respective field) together with an $A$-algebra map $\iota_{F_2}$ of finite charts inducing the inclusion on Laurent series, compatibility of $\pi_2$ with the structure maps to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and with the finite-chart immersions, equality of the preimage of the finite chart with the finite chart, finiteness of $\pi_2$ and of $\iota_{F_2}$, and the statement that $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A\,K_1\,j$ is exactly the integral closure of the image of $\iota_{F_2}$; these are summarised here. Further, $z$ is a point of the model over $K_1$ at which the germ $\varpi z$ of $\varpi$ pulled back from the base lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, $y$ is a point of the finite chart mapping to $z$, and the supersingularity hypothesis states that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $p$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the finite chart algebra with kernel the prime $y$, the value $\varphi(j)$ lies in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,p\,\Omega$, i.e. every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no nonzero affine point killed by $p$. Finally $G$ is a finite group acting on $K_1$ by semiring automorphisms, faithfully, fixing every element of $K_1$ lying in $K_2$ and with $K_2$ as exact fixed set, and acting on $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A\,K_1\,j$ compatibly with its action on $K_1$. For the ideal $\mathfrak y=y$ of the finite chart algebra, the conclusion is that the inertia subgroup $\mathfrak y.\mathrm{inertia}\,G$ is cyclic and that $p$ does not divide its cardinality.
--
--   This is the $p=2$ case of the assertion that the inertia group of the diamond action at a supersingular point of the integral model of $X(\Gamma_1(M)\cap\Gamma_0(p))$ is cyclic of order prime to $p$; classically the inertia is $\mathrm{Aut}(E_0,C_M)/\{\pm 1\}$, of order $1$, $2$ or $3$, and the congruence condition on a prime divisor $\ell$ of $M$ rules out order $2$. It feeds the general tame statement used in the analysis of the local structure of the integral models at supersingular points.
